      "title": "Zappfresh operator DSM Fresh Foods reports ₹7.3 crore profit in H1 FY27",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/companies/quarterly-results/zappfresh-operator-dsm-fresh-foods-reports-7-3-crore-profit-in-h1-fy27-126111500195_1.html",
      "description": "DSM Fresh Foods Limited, which operates the meat and seafood brand Zappfresh, has reported a consolidated net profit of Rs 7.30 crore for the first half ended September 30, 2026.\nThe company, which entered the capital markets in October 2025, had posted a net profit (after minority interest) of Rs 7.31 crore in the preceding six-month period ended March 31, 2026.\nIts total revenue from operations on a consolidated basis stood at Rs 165.48 crore in the April-September period of FY27, up 32.42 per cent compared to Rs 124.97 crore in the second half of FY26.\nIncluding other income, the total consolidated income reached Rs 165.89 crore.\nThe company's expenses during the half-year rose 35.79 per cent to Rs 155.67 crore, up from Rs 114.64 crore in the preceding six months, weighing on bottom-line growth.\nOn a standalone basis, DSM Fresh Foods posted a 25.64 per cent jump in net profit to Rs 8.83 crore for H1 FY27 compared to Rs 7.03 crore in the year-ago period.\nStandalone revenue from",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-15T06:48:15"
    },
    {
      "title": "FPIs pump ₹84,290 crore into equities in first fortnight of November",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/fpis-pump-84-290-crore-into-equities-in-first-fortnight-of-november-126111500184_1.html",
      "description": "Foreign investors made a strong comeback to Indian equities in the first fortnight of November, pumping in Rs 84,290 crore after two consecutive months of net outflows, amid a global AI sell-off, falling crude prices and attractive large-cap valuations.\nThe latest inflows follow net withdrawals of Rs 34,843 crore in October and Rs 35,861 crore in September, according to data from the depositories.\nAccording to the data,  Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPIs) made a net investment of Rs 84,290 crore in Indian equities during November 2-13, covering nine trading sessions.\nWith the latest inflows, cumulative FPI outflows from Indian equities have narrowed to around Rs 2.1 lakh crore in 2026 from nearly Rs 3 lakh crore a few weeks ago.\n\"November's foreign buying is a sharp tactical turn, not yet a structural return. Falling crude and money rotating out of the crowded Asian AI trade have pushed FPIs to close an extreme underweight on India, and most of that buying has gone into index ...",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-15T06:27:55"
    }
